<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pact of San José de Flores</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1859">1859</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Pact of Union</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Pact of San José de Flores, signed on 11 November 1859 after Urquiza's victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cepeda-1859">Battle of Cepeda</a>, ended the separation of the State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> from the Argentine Confederation. Buenos Aires agreed to rejoin the nation and accept the Constitution of 1853, subject to amendments proposed by a provincial convention, which were adopted in 1860. Conflict resumed soon after and was settled only by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pavon">Battle of Pavón</a> in 1861.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> had stood apart from the Argentine Confederation, the thirteen provinces of the interior, since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-caseros">Battle of Caseros</a> in 1852 removed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-manuel-de-rosas">Juan Manuel de Rosas</a>, refusing to endorse the San Nicolás Agreement or recognise the Constitution of 1853. The chief point of dispute was the Buenos Aires customs house, the main source of public revenue, which the city kept. Foreign powers also kept their embassies in Buenos Aires rather than in the Confederation's capital, Paraná.</p><p>Tensions grew after Liberals assassinated Nazario Benavídez, the Federalist governor of San Juan, in 1858. The election of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valentin-alsina">Valentín Alsina</a> as governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> brought war, and on 23 October 1859 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justo-jose-de-urquiza">Justo José de Urquiza</a>, President of the Confederation, defeated General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartolome-mitre">Bartolomé Mitre</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cepeda-1859">Battle of Cepeda</a>. Rather than subdue the province by force, Urquiza offered talks, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-solano-lopez">Francisco Solano López</a>, son of the President of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a>, persuaded Alsina to accept.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The talks at San José de Flores were guarded by Urquiza's troops, and Urquiza demanded the resignation of Alsina and his cabinet, who gave way to Vice Governor Felipe Llavallol. López restarted the stalled negotiations on 9 November. The final text closely followed Urquiza's stipulations: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> was declared part of the Confederation and would convene a provincial convention to propose amendments to the Constitution, to be discussed by a national convention at Santa Fe.</p><p>The province gave up its diplomatic relations and its customs house, which was nationalised, but kept its other public property, and its territory could not be divided without the consent of its legislature. A blanket amnesty was declared, and the occupying army was to withdraw from the province.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The national convention met on 14 September 1860 and approved the amendments on 23 September. Santiago Derqui had meanwhile been elected president, and the new governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartolome-mitre">Bartolomé Mitre</a>, won from him concessions on the customs house and the province's bank, which strained Derqui's relations with Urquiza. A revolt organised by Domingo Sarmiento in San Juan on 16 November, in which Governor José Antonio Virasoro was murdered, renewed the fighting. Mitre abrogated the pact, and his victory at Pavón led to his election on 4 September 1862 as first president of the reunited Argentine Republic.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pact of San José de Flores” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Urquiza defeated Buenos Aires at Cepeda in 1859, and the <b>Pact of San José de Flores</b> brought it back into the confederation after constitutional amendments.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-buenos-aires">State of Buenos Aires</a></li></ul></section>
